<section class="sec" id="sec-sim">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">04</span><h2>Simulator: Drive the Locomotive</h2></div>

  <p>The top panel is the girder with the train on it. The bottom panel plots your chosen function against train position for the <em>entire</em> traverse &mdash; that is the sawtooth, drawn exactly (these functions are piecewise linear, so the plot is not an approximation). The dot is where you are; the marker is the true maximum.</p>

  <div class="calc">
    <div class="calc-in">
      <label>What are we maximising?
        <select id="ml-mode">
          <option value="R" selected>Reaction at A</option>
          <option value="V">Shear at the section</option>
          <option value="M">Moment at the section</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label>Section at c = <span id="ml-cv">20</span> ft from A <span class="small">(used for shear &amp; moment)</span>
        <input type="range" id="ml-c" min="1" max="59" value="20">
      </label>
      <label>Train position <span class="small">&mdash; drag, or use the buttons</span>
        <input type="range" id="ml-s" min="-800" max="600" value="0">
      </label>
      <div class="calc-pair">
        <button class="mlbtn" id="ml-step">&#9664; Next wheel</button>
        <button class="mlbtn" id="ml-back">Previous &#9654;</button>
      </div>
      <button class="mlbtn mlbtn-p" id="ml-max">Jump to the maximum</button>
    </div>
    <div class="calc-out">
      <div id="ml-fig" class="calc-fig"></div>
      <div id="ml-res" class="calc-results"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal"><span class="vtag">Try this</span><h4>See the cliffs</h4><p>Stay on "Reaction at A" and drag the position slider slowly. Watch the plot climb and drop. Count the teeth &mdash; one per wheel.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb"><span class="vtag">Try this</span><h4>Walk the method</h4><p>Press <em>Next wheel</em> repeatedly from the start. The panel shows the gain, the loss and the net &Delta;. Stop when &Delta; goes red &mdash; that is the answer, and it matches Section 7.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-vio"><span class="vtag">Try this</span><h4>Move the section</h4><p>Switch to Shear and drag c from 20 ft to 10 ft. The critical wheel stays wheel 3, but the peak jumps from 75.6 to 112.4 kips &mdash; and new terms appear as loads run off the left end.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 5 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-shear">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">05</span><h2>Shear: Same Trick, New Cliff</h2></div>

  <p>Shear at a section is the reaction minus whatever load has already gone past:</p>
  <div class="eq">\[ V \;=\; R_A \;-\; \Sigma P_&#123;\text&#123;left of section&#125;&#125; \]</div>

  <p>So as the train rolls left, V climbs for exactly the reason R<sub>A</sub> climbs &mdash; and it drops off a cliff not when a wheel leaves the <em>span</em>, but when a wheel crosses the <em>section</em>, moving from the helpful side to the harmful side. Different cliff, identical algebra:</p>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Change in shear</span>
    <div class="eq">\[ \Delta V \;=\; \frac&#123;\Sigma P\, d_1&#125;&#123;L&#125; \;-\; P_1 \qquad\text&#123;and with a load coming on:&#125;\qquad \Delta V \;=\; \frac&#123;\Sigma P\, d_1&#125;&#123;L&#125; \;+\; \frac&#123;P' e&#125;&#123;L&#125; \;-\; P_1 \]</div>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem">Here <strong>P<sub>1</sub></strong> is the wheel that <em>passes the section</em>, and d<sub>1</sub> is the spacing to the wheel behind it. Same sign rule: keep moving while &Delta;V is positive; the maximum is the last position before it turns negative.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>Why the two formulas are identical is worth a moment.</strong> V and R<sub>A</sub> differ only by the loads sitting left of the section. During a move in which nothing else changes hands, that difference is constant &mdash; so the <em>change</em> in shear <em>is</em> the change in reaction, minus the one wheel that switched sides. One derivation, two functions.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>The subtle case: loads running off the left end</span>
    <p>Put the section close to A and something new happens. A load sitting <em>between the section and A</em> is being <strong>subtracted</strong> from the shear &mdash; it produces negative shear at that section. When that load rolls off the left end of the span, the penalty vanishes, so its departure <em>increases</em> the shear.</p>
    <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:.8rem">
      <div class="vcard v-rust">
        <span class="vtag">Trap 1</span>
        <h4>Do not put it in &Sigma;P</h4>
        <p>&Sigma;P is "loads on the span that <em>stay on</em>". A wheel that leaves during the move is not one of them &mdash; leave it out or you double-count it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vcard v-grn">
        <span class="vtag">Trap 2</span>
        <h4>Add its departure as a gain</h4>
        <p>Add <strong>+ P&middot;x/L</strong>, where x is how far that load still had to travel to reach A when the move began. Positive, because losing a load that was hurting you helps.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">In the section-at-10-ft example this shows up as the innocuous-looking <code class="mblk" style="display:inline;padding:.1rem .4rem">+ 10 &times; 5/60</code> and <code class="mblk" style="display:inline;padding:.1rem .4rem">+ 10 &times; 2/60</code> terms &mdash; wheel 1 and wheel 2 leaving the span while wheels 3 and 4 come up to the section.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="analogy">
    <span class="an-icon">&#9878;</span>
    <div>
      <strong>Shear is a tug of war across the cut.</strong> Loads to the right of the section pull your way through the reaction; loads to the left pull against you. A wheel crossing the section is a player <em>switching teams</em> &mdash; which is why it costs you the full P<sub>1</sub> and not half of it. A wheel dropping off at A is an opposing player leaving the field, which is why it is a gain.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 6 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-mom">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">06</span><h2>Moment: The Average-Load Criterion</h2></div>

  <p>Moment has no cliffs. Nothing switches sides discontinuously &mdash; a load crossing the section contributes zero moment about it at that instant, so M is perfectly continuous. Yet the answer is still discrete: <em>at maximum moment a wheel is at the section</em>. Understanding why gives you the most useful criterion in the chapter.</p>

  <p>Read it off the <strong>moment influence line</strong>: a triangle with its peak <em>i</em> under the section C, rising over the left arm <em>a</em> and falling over the right arm <em>b</em>.</p>

  <div class="diagram-card">
    <h4>Moving everything left: the right-hand loads climb, the left-hand loads descend</h4>
    <svg class="static-svg" style="max-width:470px" viewBox="0 0 470 192" role="img" aria-label="Moment influence line triangle with resultant W1 left of the section sliding down its slope and W2 right of it climbing toward the peak">
      <defs>
        <marker id="mlA" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 Z" fill="var(--rust)"/></marker>
        <marker id="mlB" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L7,3.5 L0,7 Z" fill="var(--teal)"/></marker>
      </defs>
      <!-- beam -->
      <line x1="40" y1="26" x2="430" y2="26" stroke="var(--txt2)" stroke-width="2"/>
      <polygon points="40,26 33,36 47,36" fill="var(--bdr2)" stroke="var(--txt2)"/>
      <polygon points="430,26 423,36 437,36" fill="var(--bdr2)" stroke="var(--txt2)"/>
      <text x="34" y="20" font-size="9" fill="var(--mut)">A</text>
      <text x="428" y="20" font-size="9" fill="var(--mut)">B</text>
      <text x="404" y="13" font-size="8.5" fill="var(--amb)" text-anchor="end">&#9664; all loads move left together</text>
      <line x1="170" y1="20" x2="170" y2="142" stroke="var(--vio)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/>
      <text x="176" y="20" font-size="9" fill="var(--vio)">C</text>
      <!-- IL triangle -->
      <path d="M40,138 L170,66 L430,138 Z" fill="var(--vdim)" stroke="var(--vio)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
      <line x1="40" y1="138" x2="430" y2="138" stroke="var(--bdr2)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="176" y="62" font-size="8.5" fill="var(--vio)">i = ab/L</text>
      <!-- W1 sliding down the left slope -->
      <text x="110" y="47" font-size="9" fill="var(--rust)" text-anchor="middle">W&#8321;</text>
      <line x1="110" y1="52" x2="110" y2="93" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2,2"/>
      <circle cx="110" cy="99" r="4" fill="var(--rust)"/>
      <line x1="104" y1="103" x2="78" y2="118" stroke="var(--rust)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#mlA)"/>
      <!-- W2 climbing the right slope -->
      <text x="300" y="47" font-size="9" fill="var(--teal)" text-anchor="middle">W&#8322;</text>
      <line x1="300" y1="52" x2="300" y2="96" stroke="var(--teal)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2,2"/>
      <circle cx="300" cy="102" r="4" fill="var(--teal)"/>
      <line x1="294" y1="100" x2="252" y2="88" stroke="var(--teal)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#mlB)"/>
      <!-- dimensions -->
      <line x1="40" y1="148" x2="170" y2="148" stroke="var(--mut)" stroke-width=".8"/>
      <line x1="170" y1="148" x2="430" y2="148" stroke="var(--mut)" stroke-width=".8"/>
      <line x1="40" y1="144" x2="40" y2="152" stroke="var(--mut)" stroke-width=".8"/>
      <line x1="170" y1="144" x2="170" y2="152" stroke="var(--mut)" stroke-width=".8"/>
      <line x1="430" y1="144" x2="430" y2="152" stroke="var(--mut)" stroke-width=".8"/>
      <text x="105" y="160" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)" text-anchor="middle">a</text>
      <text x="300" y="160" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)" text-anchor="middle">b</text>
      <!-- captions -->
      <text x="40" y="176" font-size="8.2" fill="var(--rust)">W&#8321; slides DOWN the left slope (i/a = b/L) &rarr; decrease D = W&#8321;&middot;i/a</text>
      <text x="40" y="188" font-size="8.2" fill="var(--teal)">W&#8322; climbs UP the right slope (i/b = a/L) &rarr; increase I = W&#8322;&middot;i/b</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="cap">W<sub>1</sub> is the resultant of everything left of C, W<sub>2</sub> of everything right of it. Move the train left and the two resultants slide along the two slopes in opposite directions. The moment peaks where their effects cancel.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>The change in moment</span>
    <div class="eq">\[ \Delta M \;=\; I - D \;=\; W_2\frac&#123;i&#125;&#123;b&#125; \;-\; W_1\frac&#123;i&#125;&#123;a&#125; \]</div>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>I</strong> = increase from the loads on the right climbing toward the peak. <strong>D</strong> = decrease from the loads on the left sliding down toward A. Positive &Delta;M means keep moving left.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Set &Delta;M = 0 and the criterion falls out</span>
    <div class="eq">\[ W_1\frac&#123;i&#125;&#123;a&#125; = W_2\frac&#123;i&#125;&#123;b&#125; \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; \frac&#123;W_1&#125;&#123;a&#125; = \frac&#123;W_2&#125;&#123;b&#125; = \frac&#123;W_1+W_2&#125;&#123;a+b&#125; = \boxed&#123;\;\frac&#123;W&#125;&#123;L&#125;\;&#125; \]</div>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>In words:</strong> the moment at C is a maximum when the <em>average load per foot to the left</em> of C equals the <em>average load per foot to the right</em> &mdash; which, by the same step, equals the average load per foot over the whole span. That last form is the one everybody quotes, because W and L are trivially known.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="analogy">
    <span class="an-icon">&#9878;</span>
    <div>
      <strong>Balancing load <em>density</em>, not load.</strong> Not "equal weight on both sides" &mdash; that would ignore the arms. It is equal weight <em>per foot</em>. If the right-hand stretch is more densely loaded than the span average, dragging more of it toward C still pays. Once the left is the denser side, you have overshot. Maximum moment is the crossover, and the span average W/L is the neutral reading you compare against.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Why a wheel must be sitting at the section</span>
    <p>W<sub>1</sub> and W<sub>2</sub> only change when a load crosses C. Between crossings both are constant, so &Delta;M holds one fixed sign and the moment marches steadily in one direction. It can therefore only turn from rising to falling <strong>at the instant a load passes from the right of C to the left</strong>. Same conclusion as the sawtooth, reached through a slope change instead of a cliff.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>How you actually test it</span>
    <p>Put wheel <em>k</em> at the section. The wheel itself is ambiguous &mdash; it is on the boundary &mdash; so test it both ways:</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Count wheel k on the right:</strong> the left-hand average must be <em>less</em> than the span average &nbsp; &rarr; &nbsp; W<sub>1</sub>/a &lt; W/L</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Count wheel k on the left:</strong> the left-hand average must now be <em>greater</em> &nbsp; &rarr; &nbsp; W<sub>1</sub>/a &gt; W/L</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div>Both hold &rarr; wheel <em>k</em> satisfies the criterion. Only one holds &rarr; it does not; move on.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>Two warnings the textbook is emphatic about.</strong> <strong>(1)</strong> More than one wheel can satisfy the criterion &mdash; it happens whenever loads pass off at the left or come on at the right during the move. When it does, compute M for each candidate and take the larger; the answers are usually within a percent or two, but "usually" does not sign a drawing. <strong>(2)</strong> Since maximum moment wants as much load on the span as possible, do not waste time testing the first wheel or two &mdash; start where the heavy axles are, and let the criterion do the rest.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 7 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-ex">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">07</span><h2>Worked Examples</h2></div>
  <p class="small">All four use the 14-wheel locomotive from Section 1 on a 60 ft span. Every position and value below can be reproduced in the simulator.</p>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 1</span><h4>Maximum Reaction at A</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> Find the wheel position giving the greatest reaction at A, and its value.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Wheel 1 over A &rarr; wheel 2 over A</strong> (move 5 ft). On the span and staying on: 234<sup>k</sup>. Nothing new comes on.
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta R = 234\times\tfrac&#123;5&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 10 = +9.5 \quad\text&#123;increase&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Wheel 2 &rarr; wheel 3</strong> (move 8 ft). &Sigma;P = 224<sup>k</sup>; wheel 12 (20<sup>k</sup>) rolls on by e = 1 ft.
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta R = 224\times\tfrac&#123;8&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 20\times\tfrac&#123;1&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 10 = +20.2 \quad\text&#123;increase&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Wheel 3 &rarr; wheel 4</strong> (move 6 ft). &Sigma;P = 208<sup>k</sup>; wheel 13 rolls on by e = 3 ft. Now we are losing a 36<sup>k</sup> axle instead of a 10<sup>k</sup> one:
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta R = 208\times\tfrac&#123;6&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 20\times\tfrac&#123;3&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 36 = -14.2 \quad\text&#123;decrease&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Negative &rarr; do not make that move.</strong> The previous position governs. Summing the reaction directly with wheel 3 at A: R<sub>A</sub> = 120.7 + 9.5 + 20.2 = 150.4<sup>k</sup>.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> maximum R<sub>A</sub> = <strong>150.4 kips</strong>, with <strong>wheel 3 directly over A</strong>. Note what did the work: the loss term jumped from 10 to 36 the moment a driver axle reached the support, and that alone flipped the sign. Note also that dropping the two small "rolling on" terms would not have changed any decision &mdash; but you cannot know that until you have written them down.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 2</span><h4>Maximum Positive Shear at a Section 20 ft from A</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> Same train, same span. Find the position for greatest positive shear at the section, and its value.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Wheel 1 at section &rarr; wheel 2 at section</strong> (5 ft). &Sigma;P = 174<sup>k</sup>; wheel 8 (10<sup>k</sup>) comes on by 2 ft.
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta V = 174\times\tfrac&#123;5&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 10\times\tfrac&#123;2&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 10 = +4.83 \quad\text&#123;increase&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Wheel 2 &rarr; wheel 3</strong> (8 ft). &Sigma;P = 184<sup>k</sup>; wheel 9 (20<sup>k</sup>) comes on by 1 ft.
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta V = 184\times\tfrac&#123;8&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 20\times\tfrac&#123;1&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 10 = +14.87 \quad\text&#123;increase&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Wheel 3 &rarr; wheel 4</strong> (6 ft). &Sigma;P = 204<sup>k</sup>; wheel 10 comes on by 3 ft; a 36<sup>k</sup> axle now crosses the section.
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta V = 204\times\tfrac&#123;6&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 20\times\tfrac&#123;3&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 36 = -14.6 \quad\text&#123;decrease&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div>With wheel 3 at the section, R<sub>A</sub> = 95.57<sup>k</sup> and the loads left of the section total 20<sup>k</sup> (wheels 1 and 2), so V = 95.57 &minus; 20.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> maximum V = <strong>75.6 kips</strong>, with <strong>wheel 3 at the section</strong>. Same critical wheel as Example 1, and for the same reason &mdash; the first heavy axle arriving at the critical point is what turns the sign.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 3</span><h4>Shear at a Section 10 ft from A &mdash; Loads Running Off</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> Move the section to 10 ft from A. Now wheels reach the end of the span while later wheels are still coming up to the section, so the Article 64 correction bites.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Wheel 1 &rarr; wheel 2 at the section</strong> (5 ft). &Sigma;P = 184<sup>k</sup>, wheel 9 comes on by 3 ft. Nothing leaves the span yet.
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta V = 184\times\tfrac&#123;5&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 20\times\tfrac&#123;3&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 10 = +6.33 \quad\text&#123;increase&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Wheel 2 &rarr; wheel 3</strong> (8 ft). Wheel 1 now runs off at A, so it is <em>excluded</em> from &Sigma;P (204 &minus; 10 = 194<sup>k</sup>) and its departure is <em>added</em> as a gain, +10&times;5/60, because it had 5 ft left to travel. Wheels 10 and 11 (40<sup>k</sup> together) come on by 7 and 3 ft &mdash; average 5.
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta V = 194\times\tfrac&#123;8&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 40\times\tfrac&#123;5&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 10\times\tfrac&#123;5&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 10 = +20.03 \quad\text&#123;increase&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Wheel 3 &rarr; wheel 4</strong> (6 ft). Wheel 2 runs off with 2 ft to travel; &Sigma;P = 224<sup>k</sup>; a 36<sup>k</sup> axle crosses the section.
        <div class="eq">\[ \Delta V = 224\times\tfrac&#123;6&#125;&#123;60&#125; + 10\times\tfrac&#123;2&#125;&#123;60&#125; - 36 = -13.27 \quad\text&#123;decrease&#125; \]</div></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div>With wheel 3 at the section, R<sub>A</sub> = 122.4<sup>k</sup> and only wheel 2 (10<sup>k</sup>) is left of the section.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> maximum V = <strong>112.4 kips</strong>, again with <strong>wheel 3 at the section</strong>. Compare with Example 2's 75.6 kips: the closer the section is to the support, the larger the shear &mdash; exactly what the shear influence line predicts. The two <em>+P&middot;x/L</em> terms are small here, but the wheels they represent had to be kept out of &Sigma;P, and that is a 10-kip error each if you forget.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 4</span><h4>Maximum Moment at C, 20 ft from A</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> Find the position of the train for maximum bending moment at C (a = 20 ft, b = 40 ft, L = 60 ft), and the moment there.</p>
    <p class="small">Maximum moment wants as much load on the span as possible, so start at wheel 3 &mdash; the first driver axle. Test each candidate against W/L.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Wheel 3 at C.</strong> W = 204<sup>k</sup> on the span, so W/L = 204/60 = 3.40.<br>
        Counted right: W<sub>1</sub>/a = 20/20 = 1.00 &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 3.40 &gt; 1.00 &#10003;<br>
        Counted left: W<sub>1</sub>/a = 56/20 = 2.80 &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 3.40 &gt; 2.80 &#10007; (needs to be less)<br>
        <strong>Wheel 3 fails.</strong> Too little load is behind C &mdash; keep rolling.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Wheel 4 at C.</strong> W = 224<sup>k</sup>, W/L = 3.733.<br>
        Counted right: 56/20 = 2.80 &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 3.733 &gt; 2.80 &#10003; &nbsp;&nbsp; Counted left: 92/20 = 4.60 &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 3.733 &lt; 4.60 &#10003;<br>
        <strong>Wheel 4 satisfies the criterion.</strong></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Wheel 5 at C.</strong> Wheel 1 has run off, wheel 2 now sits <em>exactly over A</em>, and wheel 11 has come on. A load standing on the support contributes nothing to the moment at C and is about to leave, so it takes no part in the criterion: W = 224<sup>k</sup>, W/L = 3.733.<br>
        Counted right: 72/20 = 3.60 &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 3.733 &gt; 3.60 &#10003; &nbsp;&nbsp; Counted left: 108/20 = 5.40 &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 3.733 &lt; 5.40 &#10003;<br>
        <strong>Wheel 5 also satisfies it</strong> &mdash; precisely the multiple-solution case the criterion warns about, caused by wheels leaving at A. Wheel 6 fails, so compute both and compare.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>&Sigma;</b><div><strong>Wheel 4 at C.</strong> R<sub>A</sub> = 2(10)(56.5/60) + 4(36)(37/60) + 2(10)(18/60) + 2(20)(5/60) = 18.83 + 88.80 + 6.00 + 3.33 = 116.97<sup>k</sup><br>
        M<sub>C</sub> = 116.97(20) &minus; [2(10)(16.5) + 36(6.0)] = 2339.3 &minus; 546 = <strong>1793.3 ft-kips</strong></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>&Sigma;</b><div><strong>Wheel 5 at C.</strong> R<sub>A</sub> = 4(36)(43/60) + 2(10)(24/60) + 3(20)(9/60) = 103.2 + 8.0 + 9.0 = 120.2<sup>k</sup><br>
        M<sub>C</sub> = 120.2(20) &minus; 2(36)(9) = 2404 &minus; 648 = <strong>1756.0 ft-kips</strong></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> maximum M<sub>C</sub> = <strong>1793.3 ft-kips</strong> with <strong>wheel 4 at the section</strong>. Wheel 5 gives 1756 &mdash; only 2% less, which is exactly why the textbook says the difference between competing candidates is usually small, and exactly why you still check both. <em>(Each grouped term above is a set of equal wheels multiplied by their average distance from the far support &mdash; a shortcut, not a new principle.)</em></div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>A note on the printed figure.</strong> The original text gives 1792 ft-kips here. It rounds each reaction term to one decimal (18.8 instead of 18.833, 3.3 instead of 3.333) before summing, which loses 0.07<sup>k</sup> off R<sub>A</sub> and 1.3 ft-kips off the moment. The exact value is 1793.3, which is what the simulator reports. Neither the critical wheel nor the design decision changes &mdash; but it is worth seeing how quickly rounded intermediates drift.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 8 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-quiz">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">08</span><h2>Quick Reference &amp; Quick Check</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>The core insight</span><span>maxima occur with a wheel at the critical point</span></div>
    <div><span>Critical point, reaction</span><span>over the support</span></div>
    <div><span>Critical point, shear &amp; moment</span><span>at the section</span></div>
    <div><span>Change in reaction</span><span>&Delta;R = &Sigma;Pd&#8321;/L &minus; P&#8321;</span></div>
    <div><span>Change in shear</span><span>&Delta;V = &Sigma;Pd&#8321;/L &minus; P&#8321;</span></div>
    <div><span>Load coming on</span><span>add + P&prime;e/L</span></div>
    <div><span>Load running off at A</span><span>add + P&middot;x/L (shear only)</span></div>
    <div><span>&Sigma;P means</span><span>loads on the span that stay on</span></div>
    <div><span>P&#8321; means</span><span>the load leaving the span / passing the section</span></div>
    <div><span>Stop rule</span><span>make moves while &Delta; &gt; 0; stop when &Delta; &lt; 0</span></div>
    <div><span>Change in moment</span><span>&Delta;M = I &minus; D = W&#8322;(i/b) &minus; W&#8321;(i/a)</span></div>
    <div><span>Moment criterion</span><span>W&#8321;/a = W&#8322;/b = W/L</span></div>
    <div><span>Criterion in words</span><span>average load left = average load right</span></div>
    <div><span>Ties</span><span>several wheels may qualify &mdash; compute each</span></div>
    <div><span>Worked answers</span><span>R<sub>A</sub> 150.4<sup>k</sup> &middot; V 75.6<sup>k</sup> &middot; M 1793 ft-k</span></div>
    <div><span>Simple load groups</span><span>inspection beats algebra &mdash; use it</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" id="quiz">
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>1. Why does the maximum reaction always occur with a wheel exactly over the support?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Because that wheel then carries the whole reaction by itself</button>
      <button data-v="1">Because the reaction climbs steadily and then drops suddenly as each wheel leaves — the peak is the instant before a drop</button>
      <button data-v="2">Because the influence line for reaction has its peak at the support</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>2. You compute &Delta;R for a move and get a negative number. What do you do?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Make the move anyway and test the next wheel — the trend may reverse</button>
      <button data-v="1">Reverse the direction of travel and start again from the other end</button>
      <button data-v="2">Do not make the move — the previous position is the maximum</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="0">
      <p>3. A wheel is going to roll off the left end of the span during your move. In &Delta;V for a section 10 ft from A, that wheel:</p>
      <button data-v="0">Is excluded from &Sigma;P, and its departure is added as a gain +P&middot;x/L</button>
      <button data-v="1">Is included in &Sigma;P like any other wheel on the span</button>
      <button data-v="2">Is subtracted twice, once from &Sigma;P and once as P&#8321;</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>4. The criterion W&#8321;/a = W&#8322;/b = W/L says the moment at C is a maximum when:</p>
      <button data-v="0">The total load left of C equals the total load right of C</button>
      <button data-v="1">The average load per foot left of C equals the average load per foot right of it</button>
      <button data-v="2">The heaviest axle sits exactly at C</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>5. Moment is a continuous function of train position — no cliffs. So why is the critical position still "a wheel at the section"?</p>
      <button data-v="0">It is not — for moment the peak generally falls between wheels</button>
      <button data-v="1">Because the moment influence line peaks at the section</button>
      <button data-v="2">Because W&#8321; and W&#8322; only change when a load crosses the section, so &Delta;M can only change sign there</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="quiz-score" class="quiz-score"></div>
</section>

